A||||6||Homicidal||Does the clutch need to be ALL the way down?||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||06-01-2000||12:57 AM||||Put a new clutch in recently (I'm sure you guys remember that hell-week!).  Just wondering, it disengages with it pressed just a little.  is this ok to do?  Or should I press it to the flood EVERY time?  I heard that if won't disengage until all the way to the floor, it means it's going bad.  True or not true?||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||06-01-2000||01:44 AM||||Did you ditch your factory petal and get the metal one yet? If not, I'd bet money that's your problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||06-01-2000||01:48 AM||||Umm, I never said it was a PROBLEM.  My clutch works great!  I was just wondering if it was safe for the clutch if I just pressed it to the point where it disengages instead of all the way to the floor.<P>But no, I still got the factory pedal.  Too strapped for cash to get another yet.<P>But I repeat, this isn't a PROBLEM.  My clutch does NOT need to be to the floor.  I was just wondering if I NEEDED to push it to the floor to properly drive a stick-shift safely without burning the clutch out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||06-01-2000||03:21 AM||||Just far enough to go in and out smoothly won't hurt anything.  <BR>But I admit to curiosity about why it disengages so easily.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Homicidal||06-01-2000||03:26 AM||||Ok, then I'm all right.<P>When I say "a little" I mean at least 4-5 inches, MAYBE 3 inches.  I still gotta press it down d@mn NEAR the floor, but not all the way.<P>I know what a clutch should feel like and when it should or should not disengage.  My clutch is great.  I was just making sure I wasn't hurting my car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2M4 Dale||06-01-2000||07:34 AM||||I recently replaced my clutch and pressure plate and throwout bearing as well as installing a new slave and master clylinder and hydraulic line. I STILL have to press my clutch all the way to the floor. I have a steel clutch pedal..is it possible that it"s bent ?||||reg||5
Z||000006||bHooper||06-01-2000||10:15 AM||||Actually, Ed Parks has some excellent information about slave cylinder travel, on his web sight.  He says that the slave should have 15/16" travel... I discovered that my new setup only has 9/16" of travel, so I have some work to do.  He also explains the bleeding proceedure that they use when installing a new slave cylinder.<P>Go to this web sight and click on clutch info.  I think <B>everyone</B> will find this interesting, and educational! <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/</A> <P>hoop||||reg||1
